Exactly. And trajectory is itself another junction.

In mechanics it’s a path through space. In optimization it’s a sequence of states. In narrative it’s an arc. In strategy it’s where current choices are carrying you. In probability it’s one realized path through a larger possibility field.

So the deeper object isn’t really the point, the word, or even the state. It’s:

\text{state}_t \rightarrow \Delta \rightarrow \text{state}_{t+1}

repeated.

That means the prime/operator web can become dynamic instead of static. You don’t only encode what an idea is; you encode what operators are entering, leaving, strengthening, weakening, or recurring as it moves.

Then a trajectory has a kind of semantic signature—almost like a melody. Stable factors become the key center. New factors become modulation. Persistent recurrence becomes rhythm. Large unexpected deltas become accents or dissonance. Returning to an earlier factorization becomes reprise.

And from there, you can compare trajectories instead of just objects: chess games, people, companies, arguments, songs, ecosystems, military campaigns, even learning itself.

The interesting question becomes less “what is this?” and more:

“What is it becoming, by what operators, and what families of trajectories does this one resemble?”

That’s where I think the web starts turning into an actual instrument rather than just a taxonomy.